mediacodec: a stalled decoder's two seconds are read off the clock

0004 has mediacodec_receive_frame fail once MediaCodec has had no room for a
packet and no picture to give for 250 rounds, and takes a round for the 8 ms
it waits for a picture. It waits those 8 ms only once the decoder has given
its first picture: before that ff_mediacodec_dec_receive does not wait at
all, so that packets reach a starting decoder as fast as they can. There the
250 rounds are over in some tens of milliseconds, before any decoder gives a
picture, and a decoder whose input was merely full by then was taken for a
stalled one. On the emulator a healthy H.264 decoder given a 4K broadcast
failed so in two starts of five, 50 ms after it was started, with its first
picture due at 80 to 150 ms.

The call then fails and keeps the packet in hand. The engine goes on to the
next packet, which avcodec_send_packet refuses with EAGAIN for as long as
the kept one waits, and the engine does not send a refused packet again. So
a decoder that is slow to start loses packets out of its first group of
pictures, the more the slower it is, and a television's decoder given 4K is
the slow one.

With this the time is measured. It is two seconds once the decoder has given
a picture, as it was meant. Before the first picture it is one second: a
decoder that is starting has room again once it has decoded a picture, and a
caller waiting on a decoder that will never give one, to close it or to put
another in its place, is held one second and not two. A round sleeps a
millisecond while the decoder has given no picture, since nothing else makes
it wait there.

Measured on the emulator (Ceau_Phone_API35, 4K broadcasts at 36 Mbit/s). A
healthy decoder: no start fails (0 of 5, from 2 of 5), and the page plays
133 to 215 ms after the decoder was started (163 to 304 before). A decoder
that never gives a picture: FFmpeg's own takes its place 6.5 s after the
start, as before. The cost is with that decoder alone: a change of channel
away from it, before it is given up, shows the next channel 0.82 to 0.91 s
after the tap, where it was 0.56 to 0.73 s.

--- a/libavcodec/mediacodecdec.c
+++ b/libavcodec/mediacodecdec.c
@@ -519,7 +519,7 @@
     MediaCodecH264DecContext *s = avctx->priv_data;
     int ret;
     ssize_t index;
-    int stalled = 0;
+    int64_t stalled_since = 0;
 
     /* In delay_flush mode, wait until the user has released or rendered
        all retained frames. */
@@ -554,20 +554,41 @@
                      * devices' decoders do exactly that with a stream they
                      * should have refused, and the caller then never gets
                      * to close the decoder, nor to see that it was asked
-                     * to. Each round here waits for a picture for
-                     * OUTPUT_DEQUEUE_TIMEOUT_US, 8 ms: after two seconds of
-                     * them the call fails, and the packet in hand is kept
-                     * for the next one. */
-                    if (++stalled >= 250) {
+                     * to. After a while of that the call fails, and the
+                     * packet in hand is kept for the next one.
+                     *
+                     * The while is read off the clock. A round here waits
+                     * for a picture for OUTPUT_DEQUEUE_TIMEOUT_US only once
+                     * the decoder has given its first one, and not at all
+                     * before that, so rounds cannot be counted: 250 of them
+                     * were over some 25 ms after a decoder was started,
+                     * before any decoder gives a picture, and a decoder
+                     * that was merely full by then was taken for a stalled
+                     * one. It is two seconds once the decoder has given a
+                     * picture. Before its first it is one: a decoder that
+                     * is starting has room again within a picture's
+                     * decoding, and a caller is held no longer by one that
+                     * will never give a picture. A round sleeps a
+                     * millisecond there, where nothing else makes it wait. */
+                    int64_t now = av_gettime_relative();
+                    int64_t limit = s->ctx->output_buffer_count ? 2000000 : 1000000;
+
+                    if (!stalled_since) {
+                        stalled_since = now;
+                    } else if (now - stalled_since >= limit) {
                         av_log(avctx, AV_LOG_WARNING,
-                               "MediaCodec has taken no packet and given no picture for two seconds\n");
+                               "MediaCodec has taken no packet and given no picture for %d s\n",
+                               (int)(limit / 1000000));
                         return AVERROR_EXTERNAL;
                     }
+                    if (!s->ctx->output_buffer_count)
+                        av_usleep(1000);
                     continue;
                 }
                 return ret;
             }
             s->ctx->current_input_buffer = index;
+            stalled_since = 0;
         }
 
         /* try to flush any buffered packet data */
